\section{Summary}

STraM (Strategic Transport Model, Bakker et al., 2025) is the starting point
of the research plan \emph{Surrogate-Embedded Stochastic Optimisation for
Resilient Multimodal Transport Networks}. Its published code calls the
commercial solver Gurobi. This report records how STraM was installed and run
on a Windows workstation with the open-source solver HiGHS, every problem met
on the way, the results of four runs, and what they mean for the planned
research.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{STraM runs fully without Gurobi.} All model types in the
        paper's base case were solved with HiGHS: the deterministic model,
        the expected-value (EV) model, the nine-scenario model with the first
        stage fixed (EEV), and the full nine-scenario stochastic programme
        (SP, 4.48 million constraints, 306 binary variables).
  \item \textbf{The stochastic programme does not reproduce the published
        objective.} HiGHS gives 2883.51 (\texteuro 288.35 billion). The
        paper and the authors' Gurobi log give 2444.53
        (\texteuro 244.5 billion), 18\% lower.
  \item \textbf{The reason was measured, not inferred.} The authors' saved
        Gurobi solution of the stochastic programme serves only 88--92\% of
        the transport demand. It was solved with a feasibility tolerance of
        $10^{-2}$ in a model whose demands are in million tonnes, and Gurobi's
        own log warns that the solution violates constraints. The HiGHS
        solutions serve 100\% (Section~\ref{sec:evidence}).
  \item \textbf{Consequence for the paper's headline comparison.} The paper
        reports a value of the stochastic solution of \texteuro 39.1 billion
        (13.8\%). With both models solved to strict feasibility it is between
        \texteuro 0.001 and \texteuro 0.41 billion (at most 0.14\%). This
        should be put to the authors before it is relied on
        (Section~\ref{sec:vss}).
  \item \textbf{A second, minor code issue.} The EV stage in
        \texttt{Main.py} passes one argument in the wrong position, which
        drops 344 constraints. Correcting it changes the EEV objective by
        0.14\% (Section~\ref{sec:evarg}).
  \item \textbf{Feasibility for surrogate work.} Confirmed at the level of
        the foundation: STraM can be installed, changed, run at full size and
        used to evaluate fixed first-stage decisions across scenarios on open
        tools. The surrogate method itself is untested
        (Section~\ref{sec:surrogate}).
\end{itemize}

