\subsection{Why the objectives differ: direct evidence}
\label{sec:evidence}

\subsubsection*{The models are the same}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Column counts are identical in every case (138{,}925 for EV;
        1{,}834{,}336 for SP), and the coefficient ranges printed by the two
        solvers agree. Row counts differ only because Pyomo writes each
        two-sided constraint as two rows in the file format used for Gurobi.
  \item The model code has not changed since the reference runs. The
        scenario file was changed on 14 November 2024 and reverted on
        3 December; the change only added rows for an extra scenario and left
        the nine scenarios' data untouched.
  \item The HiGHS EV result was reproduced on the second machine to all
        printed digits (2843.60638215).
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Gurobi's own warnings}

The Gurobi logs end with:

\begin{itemize}
  \item EV: ``max constraint violation (7.99e-03) exceeds tolerance'' and
        ``max bound violation (7.57e-03) exceeds tolerance''.
  \item SP: ``max constraint violation (8.69e-02) exceeds tolerance'' and
        ``max bound violation (7.97e-02) exceeds tolerance''.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{What the saved Gurobi solutions contain}

The repository ships the authors' result files. The stochastic-programme
file stores an objective of 2444.5269657417616, exactly the value in the log,
