\subsubsection*{The mechanism}

Demand in the model is in million tonnes. A feasibility tolerance of
$10^{-2}$ therefore lets each demand constraint fall short by ten thousand
tonnes, and Gurobi reports violations of up to 0.087 in the model's units. About
half of the roughly 1{,}250 demand constraints per period are for less than
0.02 million tonnes, so many can be left wholly or largely unserved. Unserved
demand costs nothing, which lowers the objective.

\subsubsection*{An independent lower bound}

Each of the nine scenarios was solved on its own with HiGHS, with all
constraints active (Table~\ref{tab:ws}). The mean of these optima, 2864.87,
is the cost if the future were known in advance. No plan that serves the
demand can have an expected cost below it, and the mean--CVaR objective of
the stochastic programme is never below the expected cost.

