\centering
\caption{Value of the stochastic solution.}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}Yrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Source & EEV & SP & VSS & VSS (billion euro) \\
\midrule
Paper (Gurobi, tolerance $10^{-2}$) & 2836.22 & 2444.53 & 13.81\% & 39.1 \\
HiGHS, code as published & 2887.63 & 2883.51 & 0.14\% & 0.41 \\
HiGHS, EV argument corrected & 2883.52 & 2883.51 & 0.0002\% & 0.001 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\begin{itemize}
  \item With strict feasibility the VSS is at most 0.14\%. In the corrected
        case it is smaller than the solver's optimality gap (0.008\%), so it
        cannot be told apart from zero.
  \item The published 13.8\% compares an EEV solution that serves about 99\%
        of demand with an SP solution that serves 88--92\%. Most of the gap
        is unserved demand, not better planning.
  \item Mean emissions are the same in the HiGHS EEV (corrected) and SP
        solutions in every period. The paper reports 2.2 Mt lower emissions
        for SP.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{How firm this is.} The measurements on the Gurobi solutions are
exact readings of the authors' files. The HiGHS results come from one solver
at default strictness. A Gurobi run of the stochastic programme at a tight
tolerance ($10^{-6}$) would settle the matter in a few minutes, and the
authors are best placed to make it. Until then this should be described as a
reproducibility finding to be confirmed, not as an established error.

\subsection{The misplaced argument in the EV stage}
\label{sec:evarg}

In \texttt{construct\_and\_solve\_EEV()} in \texttt{Main.py}, the original
call

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python]
construct_and_solve_model(base_data, model_instance_init, "EV",
                          risk_info, emission_cap_constraint,
                          FeasTol=10**-3)
\end{lstlisting}

passes \texttt{emission\_cap\_constraint} as the fifth positional argument,
which is \texttt{single\_time\_period}. Its value \texttt{False} is not
\texttt{None}, so \texttt{Model.py} skips the block that adds the initial
fuel-share, mode-shift, fleet-renewal and technology-diffusion constraints.

