\subsection{Conclusion on feasibility}

On this evidence, working with STraM as the base model for the surrogate
research is achievable. Everything that had to work before the research
could start now works: the model installs and runs on open tools, all of its
model types solve at full size on a workstation, a fixed first-stage decision
can be evaluated across scenarios, and each evaluation is cheap enough to
generate training data in volume.

What is confirmed is the foundation, not the surrogate method. Training a
surrogate, embedding it, and keeping solution quality (research question
RQ-B) remain open research and are not demonstrated here.

\subsection{Points to carry into the research}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Solver tolerance must be controlled and reported.} In
        STraM's units a tolerance of $10^{-2}$ changes the answer by 18\%.
        Surrogate training labels and benchmark solutions should come from
        one solver at one tight tolerance, and demand served should be
        checked on every solution.
  \item \textbf{STraM's value of the stochastic solution is close to zero
        for cost uncertainty.} WP1 uses the VSS as its benchmark. That
        remains a sound measure, but the plan should not cite STraM's 13.8\%
        as evidence that stochastic modelling pays off. With disruptions as
        the uncertainty the VSS may be very different, and establishing it is
        part of the research.
  \item \textbf{Scaling of the model's units deserves attention} when the
        model is extended: demands of a few thousand tonnes sit beside totals
        of two hundred million.
  \item \textbf{Measure before assuming scale.} HiGHS solved the stochastic
        programme at the root node. Its behaviour with more scenarios, with
        disruption recourse, or with embedded surrogates is unknown.
\end{itemize}

\section{Limitations}

\begin{itemize}
  \item No Gurobi run was made. The findings on the published values rest on
        the authors' files and on HiGHS.
  \item The demand served by the workstation's own stochastic solution was
        not measured directly. It is supported by the lower bound, by the
        agreement of its first-stage costs with the single-scenario optima,
        and by the 100\% measured in a diagnostic HiGHS solve of the
        deterministic model.
  \item HiGHS was given STraM's loose value as its linear-programming
        tolerance while its integer feasibility tolerance stayed at the tight
        default. It did not exploit the loose value, but the settings should
        be made explicitly tight.
  \item The emission cap was switched off in all runs.
  \item Solve-time comparisons are across different hardware, and peak
        memory was not recorded.
  \item The diagnostic runs used newer package versions than the
        workstation. Where the same model was solved on both, the objectives
        agreed to all printed digits.
\end{itemize}

\section{Next steps}
\label{sec:next}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Make the tolerances explicit.} In \texttt{solve\_model()},
        set both \texttt{primal\_feasibility\_tolerance} and
        \texttt{mip\_feasibility\_tolerance} to $10^{-6}$ regardless of the
        value passed in, and rerun the stochastic programme to confirm the
        objective does not move.
  \item \textbf{Add a demand-served check} that prints, for each period, the
        total path flow against total demand after every solve.
  \item \textbf{Write to the STraM authors} with Table~\ref{tab:served}, the
        lower bound and the argument at line 200, and ask for a Gurobi run
        at a tight tolerance.
  \item \textbf{Carbon price sensitivity} on the deterministic case with
        \texttt{co2\_fee} set to \texttt{"base"}, \texttt{"intermediate"} and
        \texttt{"high"}.
  \item \textbf{A first recourse data set}: fix several different
        first-stage decisions, evaluate each across the nine scenarios, and
        store first-stage decision, scenario and second-stage cost.
  \item \textbf{A first surrogate test} on that data set, outside the
        optimisation model.
\end{enumerate}


